@LauraSack Thank you for the suggestion. I'll add it to the backlog ;) 
My workflow right now is -> see something -> read about it -> make a video about it. That makes me a master of not one topic specifically but it does help with my understanding of the historical method in general. 

As for your question based on the limited knowledge I have now i think it's a combination of not having the wealth, the markets, and the legal frameworks to jump directly into the industrialized chattel system that would later come to define the American South. I remember a statistic that in the beginning European indentured servants outnumbered and worked together with black slaves and only later (because of capitalism) merchants decided to reinvest their earnings in order to build out the slave system (just because it was racial and cheaper than relying on indentured servants). But not 100% sure about it.
